# Array indexing in a hot loop.
#
# INDEX_GET had no case in emit_op at all, so it reached the default bail. Since
# both callables and the top level compile all-or-nothing, a single `a[i]`
# anywhere disqualified its whole enclosing body - and indexing appears in most
# non-trivial programs. It was the largest single blocker in the suite: 53 of
# 136 tests lost native compilation to it, more than DIV and USE_LIB together.
#
# Measured with tools/sura_ab_bench.ps1 -Jit, 10 interleaved rounds:
#   INDEX_GET bailing : 12.96 ms   (top level compiled 0 opcodes)
#   INDEX_GET emitted :  4.08 ms   (top level compiled 16 opcodes)
#   10/10 rounds, 3.2x
#
# Run with --jit and check the [JIT-OPS] line: INDEX_GET must appear. If it does
# not, the emitter has regressed and this benchmark will show it as a ~3x
# slowdown.

data is [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]

runs is 5
total is 0

repeat runs do
  t0 is clock()
  acc is 0
  i is 0
  while i < 300000 do
    acc is acc + data[i % 8]
    i is i + 1
  end
  t1 is clock()
  total is total + (t1 - t0) * 1000
end

avg is total / runs

# Pinned so a miscompiled index - a wrong element, a mishandled wrap - shows up
# as a wrong answer rather than only as a timing change.
assert_eq(acc, 1162500)

print "checksum: {acc}"
print "avg ({runs} runs): {avg} ms"
